ame class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: eye (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: center (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: up (3) private  subroutine set_default_view_distance (self, distance) The radius to frame when the view is reset (<= 0: the viewed frame's\nbound, the default), as OpenFrames' default view distance. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: distance radius (<= 0: the frame's bound) private  subroutine set_default_view_parameters (self, eye, center, up) The camera after a reset, in view-frame coordinates (overrides the\ndefault view distance). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: eye (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: center (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: up (3) private  subroutine set_orthographic (self, left, right, bottom, top) An orthographic projection with the given bounds (in view units). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: left real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: right real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: bottom real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: top private  subroutine set_perspective (self, fovy) A perspective projection with vertical field of view fovy (degrees; default 45). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: fovy vertical field of view (degrees) private  subroutine set_view_between_frames (self, root, frame, lookat, frame_type, rotation_type) Look from frame at lookat (both in the tree of root ). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self class( ReferenceFrame ), intent(in), target :: root class( ReferenceFrame ), intent(in), target :: frame class( ReferenceFrame ), intent(in), target :: lookat integer, intent(in), optional :: frame_type ABSOLUTE_FRAME or RELATIVE_FRAME (default) integer, intent(in), optional :: rotation_type VIEW_DIRECT or VIEW_AZEL (default) private  subroutine set_view_frame (self, root, frame, frame_type) View frame (in the tree of root ) with base frame_type (default RELATIVE_FRAME). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self class( ReferenceFrame ), intent(in), target :: root class( ReferenceFrame ), intent(in), target :: frame integer, intent(in), optional :: frame_type ABSOLUTE_FRAME or RELATIVE_FRAME private  subroutine view_set_camera (self, eye, center, up) The camera (view-frame coordinates) to show the next time the view is\ndrawn (at once if it is shown: the window's set_camera can also fly\nthere). It replaces a pending reset. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( View ), intent(inout) :: self real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: eye (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: center (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: up (3)","tags":"","url":"module/vkf_view.html"},{"title":"vkf_window – vkframes","text":"Windows showing a scene of frames, with its simulation time, as OpenFrames'\nFrameManager and WindowProxy (for now with a single viewport, which holds\nthe views as OpenFrames' RenderRectangle does; OpenFrames' grid of\nviewports comes later). A FrameManager holds the root frame of a scene. A WindowProxy shows one: in\na window of its own, or in a host's native window (e.g. a Qt QWindow's winId() ), in which case the host forwards the window's events\n( mouse , scroll , key , resize , close ). Each frame() advances the\nsimulation clock, updates the frames (followed trajectories), copies their\npositions and attitudes to the renderer, places the camera for the current\nView and draws. Several windows can show one scene (its render nodes are shared). The scene\nis updated once per simulation time: a window whose time is the one the\nscene was last updated for, by another window since this one's last frame,\ndoesn't update it again ( FrameManager%update ). Windows whose clocks are\nsynchronized ( SimulationClock%synchronize ) render the same time, their\nmaster's at its last frame, so they show the scene in the same state and\nupdate it once per round of frames, also while the time is paused (then\neach round still updates once, for data added meanwhile). A window has a list of views ( add_view , select_view , next_view , ...);\neach keeps its own camera (the trackball) while another one is shown.\nWithout views, a default view of the scene's root frame is used. Keyboard and mouse (OpenFrames' WindowProxy callbacks, setKeyPressCallback,\nsetMouseMotionCallback, setButtonPressCallback, setButtonReleaseCallback):\na program's WindowEventHandler, an extension overriding the procedures it\nwants ( key_press , key_release , mouse_motion , button_press , button_release ), is given the window's events ( set_event_handler ),\nalso those a host forwards. They're passed on after the frame in which they\narrived (from frame() ), so a handler may change anything, the window\nincluded (its views, its time, closing it). Positions are in -1 to 1 across\nthe window, y up, as OpenFrames'. The built-in keys, as OpenFrames': v the\nnext view, V the previous one, the space bar resets the view (unless the\nhandler's key_press says it handled the key, or set_default_keys(.false.) ).\nThe mouse moves the camera (the trackball); the keys don't. Each frame, the handler's before_draw(window, t) is called once the\nscene is at the frame's time and the camera is placed (the trackball's\nmoves included), just before drawing: what it changes (HUD text, labels,\nframes' positions) is in this frame, e.g. a callout kept next to a moving\nobject ( screen_position ) without lagging a frame behind, whoever runs\nthe frame loop (the program or a host GUI). The camera (OpenFrames' trackball getTransformation / setTransformation): get_camera and set_camera (eye, center, up in the shown view's frame,\nor in its root frame with in_root ). Smooth transitions (not in\nOpenFrames): set_camera with a duration flies there, and with set_view_transition(seconds) (default 0: a cut, as OpenFrames) changing\nthe view (select_view, next_view, previous_view, the keys v and V; or with\ntheir own duration ) and resetting it (the space bar) fly from the camera\nshown to the new one. A flight moves the center along a straight line, the\ndistance geometrically and the orientation by the shortest rotation, eased\nin and out, in the root frame (both views' frames as they move), over the\nwall-clock time given (the video's while recording); a mouse press, stop_transition or another flight\nends it. Video recording (not in OpenFrames, which has only screenshots): start_recording(file, fps) ... stop_recording() records the frames\ndrawn to a video file, encoded by ffmpeg (conda-forge's, in the pixi\nenvironment; or ffmpeg , or VKFRAMES_FFMPEG): the frames are piped to it\nas raw pixels (no image files; see vkf_viewer_record_start in\nsrc/vsg/vkf_layer.cpp). While recording, the window's time is the video's:\neach frame advances the simulation time by exactly scale/fps, and camera\nflights and animated shaders (pulsing markers, highlighted lines) by\n1/fps s, so the video plays smoothly at its own speed however long a\nframe takes to draw (or to encode). Stopping goes back to the wall clock\nfrom where the video was. A watermark (e.g. a logo) can be drawn over the\nvideo by ffmpeg ( watermark ). The video's size is its first frame's (a window\nresized while recording is cropped or padded). The mouse's sensitivity: set_zoom_speed (the zoom per wheel or trackpad\nstep and per right drag; default 1, VSG's) and set_camera_throw (the\ncamera keeps moving after a drag released while moving; default on). Picking (not in OpenFrames): pick(x, y, hits) gives what is under a\npoint of the window (x, y as the event handler's: -1 to 1, y up), nearest\nfirst, as drawn at the last frame: trajectory curves (the point and the\ntime there), segments and markers (within a few pixels: tolerance , plus\nhalf a line's width or a marker's size), bodies (the point on the surface,\nwith latitude and longitude) and frames' origins. Each frame of the scene\ntests its own contents (ReferenceFrame%pick, vkf_pick); nothing is done\nuntil pick is called. cursor_ray(x, y, origin, direction) gives the ray\nfrom the eye through a point of the window (root frame), e.g. to place\nsomething where the cursor points (vkf_pick's ray_plane, ray_sphere,\nray_line). Handles (vkf_handles: DragHandle, e.g. VectorHandle): while a scene shown\nin a window has handles, the window looks for one under the cursor at each\nmouse move (a pick of PICK_HANDLE only), hovers it (drawn brighter) and\narms a grab: a left button press then grabs the handle instead of moving\nthe camera, the moves drag it (DragHandle%drag, with the cursor's ray) and\nthe release lets it go. The handler is told ( handle_drag : DRAG_START,\nDRAG_MOVE, DRAG_END), not given those presses, moves and releases\n( button_press , mouse_motion , button_release ). A handle is dragged\nfrom the frame after the event's, as other events are handled. The\nmiddle and right buttons move the camera over handles too. Uses vkf_view iso_c_binding vkf_math vkf_handles vkf_clock vkf_text vkf_sky iso_fortran_env vkf_hud vkf_diagnostics vkf_pick vkf_gui vkf_frames vkf_layer module~~vkf_window~~UsesGraph module~vkf_window vkf_window iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~vkf_window->iso_c_binding iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~vkf_window->iso_fortran_env module~vkf_clock vkf_clock module~vkf_window->module~vkf_clock module~vkf_diagnostics vkf_diagnostics module~vkf_window->module~vkf_diagnostics module~vkf_frames vkf_frames module~vkf_window->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_gui vkf_gui module~vkf_window->module~vkf_gui module~vkf_handles vkf_handles module~vkf_window->module~vkf_handles module~vkf_hud vkf_hud module~vkf_window->module~vkf_hud module~vkf_layer vkf_layer module~vkf_window->module~vkf_layer module~vkf_math vkf_math module~