~vkf_window->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_window->module~vkf_handles module~vkf_window->module~vkf_hud module~vkf_window->module~vkf_view module~vkf_artists->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_handles->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_handles->module~vkf_artists module~vkf_hud->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_line_segments->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_model->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_view->module~vkf_frames Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Functions public  function find_data_file (name) result(path) The path of data file name : name itself if it exists (a path, or a\nfile in the current directory), else the first match in the directories\nof the VKFRAMES_DATA_PATH environment variable; '' if not found. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: name Return Value character(len=:), allocatable public  function find_in_dirs (name, dirs) result(path) The path dir/name of the first directory in dirs (separated by ';' or\n':', except a ':' after a drive letter) that has name , or ''. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: name file name character(len=*), intent(in) :: dirs directories, separated by ';' or ':' Return Value character(len=:), allocatable","tags":"","url":"module/vkf_files.html"},{"title":"vkf_handles – vkframes","text":"Handles (not in OpenFrames): things in the scene the mouse drags, to\ndesign graphically, e.g. a trajectory's initial position and velocity. A DragHandle is a frame with a point the mouse can grab ( handle_point ,\nin the frame's coordinates) and move ( move_handle_point ); extensions say\nwhat the point is and how they are drawn. Windows drive them (WindowProxy,\nsee vkf_window): the handle under the cursor is hovered (drawn brighter); a\nleft button press on it grabs it (the camera stays where it is: the\ntrackball doesn't see the press), the mouse's moves drag it, and the\nrelease lets it go. The window's event handler is told ( handle_drag ,\nwith DRAG_START, DRAG_MOVE and DRAG_END), e.g. to compute a trajectory again\nfrom the new state while the handle is dragged. Handles can also be moved\nby the program (VectorHandle%set_vector), and dragged by it ( grab , drag and release , given the cursor's ray). Where the point goes is where the cursor's ray (WindowProxy%cursor_ray)\nmeets the handle's constraint ( set_constraint ), as it was when the\nhandle was grabbed, plus the point's offset from the cursor then (so the\npoint doesn't jump to the cursor):\n* HANDLE_FREE (the default): the plane through the point facing the camera;\n* HANDLE_PLANE: the plane through the point with the normal given;\n* HANDLE_LINE: the line through the point along the direction given;\n* HANDLE_RADIAL: the line from the frame's origin through the point (e.g.\n  a vector's length only);\n* HANDLE_SPHERE: the sphere about the frame's origin through the point\n  (e.g. a vector's direction only), on the side (near or far, from the\n  camera) where it was grabbed; where the ray misses it, its edge nearest\n  the ray.\nNormals and directions are in the frame's coordinates. A ray parallel to a\nplane or a line doesn't move the point. Positions go through the frames'\nfirst parents to the root (ReferenceFrame%world_matrix). Handles on the same spot of the screen (e.g. a trajectory's end marker on\nits state's arrow): the window hovers the one nearest the cursor on the\nscreen; within a few pixels of each other, the one of the highest\npriority ( set_priority ; markers, TimeHandle, 1 by default, before\narrows, 0), then the nearest to the eye. A VectorHandle is an arrow from its frame's origin to its tip, the tip\nbeing the handle: a vector ( set_vector , in the frame's coordinates)\ntimes a display scale ( set_display_scale : e.g. 1000 s to draw a velocity\nin km/s as km). Dragging the tip changes the vector. The arrow is in the\nframe's color, lit as frames' axes (by how it faces the viewer); its head\nis set_head_size long (scene units; default 0.2 of the arrow, at most\nhalf of it). It is built once, as a unit shaft and head scaled and turned\nby two transforms: a new vector only sets them (cheap enough for every\nframe of a drag). A TimeHandle is a marker at a time along a path (a TimePath: a position\nfor any time, in the coordinates of the handle's parent frame, e.g. a\npropagator's), dragged along the path to change the time: e.g. a\ntrajectory's end, dragged to make it longer or shorter in time. The path\nneedn't be drawn where the handle goes (beyond a trajectory's last point,\nthe program propagates further). The frame is placed at the path's\nposition for the time ( set_time ), so its name label (e.g. the time, set_name ) goes with it; the marker is the size of MarkerArtist's\nmarkers (window units, not shrinking with distance). A drag takes the time\nwithin set_time_range whose point is nearest the cursor's ray (as seen\nfrom the eye): among the path's points sampled at the grab, then refined\nbetween them; where the path passes near itself (orbits), the match\nnearest in time to the handle's time is taken, so it doesn't jump to\nanother revolution. A TrajectoryPath follows a Trajectory's points\n(interpolated linearly), within its times. Uses iso_c_binding vkf_math vkf_artists vkf_trajectory vkf_geometry vkf_pick vkf_frames vkf_layer module~~vkf_handles~~UsesGraph module~vkf_handles vkf_handles iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~vkf_handles->iso_c_binding module~vkf_artists vkf_artists module~vkf_handles->module~vkf_artists module~vkf_frames vkf_frames module~vkf_handles->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_geometry vkf_geometry module~vkf_handles->module~vkf_geometry module~vkf_layer vkf_la