e opposite, the identity if either is zero. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: from (3) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: to (3) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4) public pure function quat_multiply (a, b) result(q) The product a*b: rotating by b, then by a. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: a (4) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: b (4) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4) public pure function quat_normalize (q) result(u) q / |q| (the identity for a zero quaternion). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q (4) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4) public pure function quat_rotate (q, v) result(r) The vector v rotated by the unit quaternion q. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q (4) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: v (3) Return Value real(kind=wp), (3) public pure function quat_slerp (t, a, b) result(q) Spherical linear interpolation from a (t = 0) to b (t = 1), along the\nshorter arc; linear when the two are nearly equal. Same as OpenSceneGraph's\nosg::Quat::slerp, which OpenFrames' TrajectoryFollower uses. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: t the fraction of the way (0 to 1) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: a (4) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: b (4) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4) public pure function quat_to_matrix (q) result(m) The 3x3 rotation matrix of the unit quaternion q (columns: the rotated axes). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: q (4) Return Value real(kind=wp), (3,3) public pure function rigid_inverse (m) result(inv) The inverse of a rigid transform (rotation and translation): [R' -R't]. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: m (4,4) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4,4) public pure function translation_matrix (t) result(m) The 4x4 transform translating by t . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: t (3) Return Value real(kind=wp), (4,4) Subroutines public pure subroutine set_rgba (rgba, color) Set rgba from color : [r, g, b] (alpha unchanged) or [r, g, b, a]. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(inout) :: rgba (4) [r, g, b, a] real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: color (:) [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, a] (0 to 1)","tags":"","url":"module/vkf_math.html"},{"title":"vkf_shadow – vkframes","text":"Shadow cones: the regions of a celestial body's shadow in the light of\nanother (e.g. the Earth's or the Moon's shadow in sunlight), drawn as\ntransparent cones: the umbra (total eclipse: the light hidden), converging behind the body\n  to its apex, at d R / (Rs - R) from the body's center; the antumbra (annular eclipse: the light seen around the body), from the\n  umbra's apex outward, widening at the umbra's angle; the penumbra (partial eclipse), widening behind the body. (R and Rs: the body's and the light's radii; d: the distance between their\ncenters. The edges are the lines tangent to both spheres: the outer\ntangents for the umbra and antumbra, half-angle asin((Rs - R) / d), the\ninner ones for the penumbra, asin((Rs + R) / d).) A body larger than its\nlight has no apex: its umbra widens (or is a cylinder), with no antumbra. A ShadowCones is a frame of its own, made a child of the body ( add_child )\nfor one body's shadow, and shown or hidden with show_contents : a scene\nwithout one does no shadow work at all. It refers to the body and the light\nframes (they must stay alive while set); each scene update, after the whole\ntree has moved (late_update), it moves itself to the body's center and\nturns its +z axis away from the light (its own position and attitude are\nset there, whatever its parent), and reshapes the cones for the distance\nbetween them: the cones' vertices are rewritten in place and uploaded only\nwhen the shape changed (not as the body only turns). Hidden, it does\nnothing (and is placed again when shown). The radii: given ( set_body_radius , set_light_radius ), or the frames'\nown: a Sphere's equatorial radius, a TerrainBody's (a light that is a plain\nReferenceFrame needs its radius given: none, no shadow). The shadow is drawn to set_length behind the body's center (default: twice the umbra's length;\na hundred body radii without an apex); the umbra stops there if shorter.\nEach region has a color ([r, g, b, a]; the surfaces are unlit,\ntransparent, seen from both sides, as the cones of vkf_shapes) and can be\ndrawn or not ( set_regions ). Bodies in a shadow are not darkened (that needs shadows in the lighting). Uses iso_c_binding vkf_math vkf_terrain vkf_sphere vkf_frames vkf_layer module~~vkf_shadow~~UsesGraph module~vkf_shadow vkf_shadow iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~vkf_shadow->iso_c_binding module~vkf_frames vkf_frames module~vkf_shadow->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_layer vkf_layer module~vkf_shadow->module~vkf_layer module~vkf_math vkf_math module~vkf_shadow->module~vkf_math module~vkf_sphere vkf_sphere module~vkf_shadow->module~vkf_sphere module~vkf_terrain vkf_terrain module~vkf_shadow->module~vkf_terrain module~vkf_frames->iso_c_binding module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_layer module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_math module~vkf_follower vkf_follower module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_follower module~vkf_geometry vkf_geometry module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_geometry module~vkf_pick vkf_pick module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_pick module~vkf_text vkf_text module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_text module~vkf_trajectory vkf_trajectory module~vkf_frames->module~vkf_trajectory module~vkf_layer->iso_c_binding iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~vkf_math->iso_fortran_env module~vkf_sphere->iso_c_binding module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_frames module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_layer module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_math module~vkf_files vkf_files module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_files module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_geometry module~vkf_sphere->module~vkf_pick module~vkf_terrain->iso_c_binding module~